WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.019 --> 00:00:01.518
Love of Siam

00:00:02.599 --> 00:00:04.004
Musical:

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:08.249
"We probably can't be in a relationship with Mew, but that doesn't mean we don't love Mew."

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.578
Back then, the conditions for love

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.709
were much more numerous.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.188
Actually, love is just about two people loving each other.

00:00:15.189 --> 00:00:17.658
If it were Tong and Mew today,

00:00:17.659 --> 00:00:19.029
maybe they could still be together.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:22.179
But I understand that even if you're not dating, you still love Mew.

00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:23.638
That's real, love really exists.

00:00:23.639 --> 00:00:28.189
The interesting thing

00:00:28.385 --> 00:00:30.544
about the musical now, back

00:00:30.746 --> 00:00:33.744
then , was that the audience was listening at a time when people were

00:00:33.745 --> 00:00:35.665
more open-minded.

00:00:35.666 --> 00:00:36.944
There's more freedom in this area

00:00:36.945 --> 00:00:39.904
, there are laws supporting louder voices and things like that.

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:43.354
There might still be people whose families have these conditions,

00:00:43.355 --> 00:00:44.545
who aren't accepting yet.

00:00:44.546 --> 00:00:47.095
But we've come a long way, and we can go even further.

00:00:47.096 --> 00:00:49.385
After working together as partners for a while,

00:00:49.386 --> 00:00:53.054
we have this beautiful young lady who is our beloved daughter,

00:00:53.416 --> 00:00:56.371
a source of happiness for me.

00:00:56.462 --> 00:00:58.011
I never thought, when

00:00:58.301 --> 00:01:00.980
they said there would be mascots for each person,

00:01:00.981 --> 00:01:03.781
that it would reach this point, where she's

00:01:03.782 --> 00:01:06.691
out there, talking, and living with us.

00:01:06.692 --> 00:01:09.371
It's like we have a real child.

00:01:09.372 --> 00:01:11.901
Sometimes it's not even during our work hours,

00:01:11.902 --> 00:01:14.231
but she's always with us, always by my side.

00:01:14.232 --> 00:01:19.780
She once sent me a video saying that even if no one in the world loves Barney, there's still

00:01:19.898 --> 00:01:21.067
me.

00:01:21.068 --> 00:01:23.147
I think there are millions of people in the world who have that.

00:01:23.148 --> 00:01:25.696
Not a million people will ever like us.

00:01:25.697 --> 00:01:27.997
Imagine if we lived to be 80

00:01:27.998 --> 00:01:30.946
, or maybe we wouldn't know when we'd die.

00:01:30.947 --> 00:01:34.296
We're constantly counting down the days until our lives end.

00:01:34.297 --> 00:01:36.796
Giving value to things like

00:01:37.138 --> 00:01:39.967
that is like wasting our time.

00:01:39.968 --> 00:01:41.656
No matter how much you try to explain,

00:01:41.657 --> 00:01:43.537
if people don't believe you

00:01:43.538 --> 00:01:46.322
or love you, they'll find reasons not to.

00:01:46.323 --> 00:01:50.883
I don't want to waste my time explaining to lazy people.

00:01:50.973 --> 00:01:55.532
For me, standing firm in myself means being confident in my own feelings and my true self.

00:01:55.533 --> 00:02:01.243
I don't want people to change me in just a day or two, after criticizing me.

00:02:01.244 --> 00:02:02.652
I've built myself up for 10 years;

00:02:02.653 --> 00:02:06.413
why should I change for just a few opinions?

00:02:06.414 --> 00:02:07.932
There are many people who love me,

00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:11.383
many who love me for who I am.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.428
You're watching Five Me

00:02:19.429 --> 00:02:21.129
, and these are five things that make up

00:02:21.289 --> 00:02:23.319
Bonnie, the foodie, the adorable,

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.819
big-eyed girl

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:36.668
who wants a fun, green drink: Popcorn Orange and Popcorn Green Grape. Drink it and instantly feel the fun!

00:02:37.429 --> 00:02:39.099
That's the right kind of deliciousness

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:46.028
. Fun, sweet, fragrant, refreshing, with a Move Pop texture that's satisfying with every bite, plus it's beneficial with

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.359
1000mg of Vitamin C.

00:02:48.419 --> 00:02:52.069
It also helps support the normal functioning of the immune system.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:56.239
Available now at CJ Mall and Jiffy stores nationwide

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.319
for only $15 per bottle.

00:03:01.399 --> 00:03:04.129
Okay, so here's my foodie sidekick, big-eyed sweetheart, and

00:03:04.729 --> 00:03:06.228
three other things

00:03:06.449 --> 00:03:07.948
! First, I'm definitely

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.769
a foodie – a big eater and I love spicy

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.178
food. I eat a wide variety of things and I'm a good eater.

00:03:14.199 --> 00:03:17.129
What kind of food am I into right now?

00:03:17.369 --> 00:03:19.879
I love Thai food, especially Som Tum (papaya salad). I love spicy food,

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.519
especially

00:03:22.649 --> 00:03:24.888
with fermented fish sauce. Fermented fish sauce is

00:03:24.889 --> 00:03:26.638
my go-to!

00:03:28.369 --> 00:03:29.868
Sticky rice with chicken... I love

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.249
spicy food, especially Thai food.

00:03:34.369 --> 00:03:36.368
Some people say my face looks like

00:03:36.369 --> 00:03:39.228
I shouldn't eat fermented fish sauce, but I actually like it!

00:03:42.979 --> 00:03:44.069
Okay,

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:48.849
next is being clingy.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.678
Sometimes, if we're close, I'll be a little

00:03:54.679 --> 00:03:56.178
affectionate. And big, round

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.538
eyes... that's what I mean because I

00:03:58.929 --> 00:04:00.508
have really big, round eyes

00:04:00.509 --> 00:04:03.269
. It's a nickname people use for my big, round eyes.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.978
When I'm acting in series, my eyes are like, "

00:04:05.139 --> 00:04:07.239
Pearl, pearl," "Big,

00:04:08.429 --> 00:04:09.928
round

00:04:11.169 --> 00:04:12.668
eyes," "

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.909
Don't leave me, P'Mee!" (a

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:19.868
nickname for a handsome,

00:04:19.869 --> 00:04:21.558
fair-skinned actor).

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:24.428
Okay,

00:04:24.429 --> 00:04:27.208
and I really like the name Bonnie.

00:04:27.579 --> 00:04:30.518
Actually, it's from Barney, right?

00:04:30.529 --> 00:04:33.358
I wanted to tell you a little about how it came about at home.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:38.148
My sister really liked watching the Barney cartoon, the purple dinosaur

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:39.939
with the green belly.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:42.988
Honestly, when I watched it,

00:04:42.989 --> 00:04:45.879
I didn't really understand what it was about, but I just knew that it

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.539
liked to take kids on adventures, it was kind,

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.719
and it sang songs.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.139
When I was little, I just enjoyed watching it. My mom said that

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:56.268
when she put on this show, everyone would be sucked in, like they were

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:59.268
concentrated on it.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.449
I want to know what kind of lessons Barney

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:03.798
actually taught Bonnie.

00:05:03.829 --> 00:05:07.879
Barney really teaches about love, doesn't it? Like,

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.108
loving each other,

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:11.608
"We are a happy family,"

00:05:11.609 --> 00:05:16.469
bright and cheerful, taking kids on adventures to learn about the new world.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:20.278
When I was a kid, watching it was like opening up a little world, like it was cute and enjoyable

00:05:20.529 --> 00:05:22.178
to watch, more

00:05:22.179 --> 00:05:24.278
for relaxation and stress relief.

00:05:24.369 --> 00:05:25.379
But it also inspired me,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.488
like I wanted to go to different places.

00:05:27.619 --> 00:05:30.249
It made me interested in the world

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.398
, wanting to explore it more.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.909
Actually, does Bonnie like adventure or exploring the world?

00:05:37.059 --> 00:05:41.099
Actually, entering the entertainment industry was also about exploring myself.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.928
Trying to step out of my comfort zone and do things like this.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.099
Because besides acting,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.648
Bonnie has also explored another role: being a singer now.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:53.569
An artist under Riser Records.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.078
I sing a lot, that's true

00:05:57.170 --> 00:06:02.759
. Actually, I've heard you sing for Art, the one who played guitar.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.058
Yes, back then you played guitar.

00:06:05.559 --> 00:06:09.569
I want to talk about pursuing these dreams together, right

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.889
? With the acting and being an artist part.

00:06:12.369 --> 00:06:13.778
Actually, since I was a kid,

00:06:13.779 --> 00:06:16.078
what I really liked was singing.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.449
Acting wasn't in my head,

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:21.889
or it was something I didn't want to do because I felt it was difficult.

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:24.793
I was afraid of many things about being an actress.

00:06:24.794 --> 00:06:25.393
But

00:06:25.394 --> 00:06:29.992
when I had the opportunity to become an actress and try entering that world,

00:06:30.093 --> 00:06:31.393
there were so many

00:06:31.394 --> 00:06:33.432
special things. It's a

00:06:33.433 --> 00:06:36.043
very special art form in acting.

00:06:36.273 --> 00:06:38.592
Like seeing people...

00:06:38.593 --> 00:06:40.742
We see actors performing in dramas

00:06:40.743 --> 00:06:43.472
and series, and it might seem entertaining, but it's actually

00:06:43.473 --> 00:06:46.182
very difficult to portray those emotions

00:06:46.183 --> 00:06:47.943
for people to see.

00:06:47.944 --> 00:06:49.693
I like things that challenge me.

00:06:49.694 --> 00:06:51.752
I feel like I'm starting to enjoy acting,

00:06:51.753 --> 00:06:53.746
like getting new roles

00:06:53.898 --> 00:06:56.527
that are very challenging. I've always liked

00:06:56.528 --> 00:06:58.017
experimenting,

00:06:58.018 --> 00:07:03.057
so this has become a new passion for me since I entered the industry.

00:07:03.518 --> 00:07:05.777
Like Barney, he keeps going,

00:07:05.778 --> 00:07:07.807
and we've made connections

00:07:10.167 --> 00:07:12.376
. And how is it different for artists?

00:07:12.578 --> 00:07:15.547
As an artist, I've loved singing since I was a child

00:07:15.548 --> 00:07:21.726
, but I lacked confidence. When I entered the industry, people started to see that I could sing,

00:07:21.907 --> 00:07:23.001
and

00:07:23.002 --> 00:07:25.341
I began to gain more confidence. I

00:07:25.342 --> 00:07:27.081
started to dare to sing,

00:07:27.082 --> 00:07:30.991
to use my own techniques.

00:07:30.992 --> 00:07:33.510
It's something I really love,

00:07:33.601 --> 00:07:37.190
but I never thought it would become a real career.

00:07:37.191 --> 00:07:40.051
Many people think singing is just

00:07:40.052 --> 00:07:42.031
a hobby or something like that,

00:07:42.032 --> 00:07:43.440
but in the end, it

00:07:43.441 --> 00:07:45.450
became my real profession, and I'm so happy

00:07:45.682 --> 00:07:47.920
because I love what I love.

00:07:47.921 --> 00:07:49.450
I can do it as much as I want without getting bored.

00:07:49.592 --> 00:07:52.544
The moment the record label contacted me to become an artist

00:07:52.545 --> 00:07:54.044
, I felt so

00:07:56.226 --> 00:07:56.824
happy,

00:07:56.825 --> 00:07:59.535
so incredibly happy, because it was a dream

00:07:59.536 --> 00:08:01.255
I hadn't dared to dream of

00:08:01.256 --> 00:08:04.345
. I wasn't the type of person who would confidently say, "

00:08:04.346 --> 00:08:06.454
Hey, I can sing!" or anything like that.

00:08:06.455 --> 00:08:08.854
But in the end, the adults saw that we

00:08:09.066 --> 00:08:10.144
could sing.

00:08:10.145 --> 00:08:13.225
Maybe it was a good thing that they saw P'Mee (Mimi) could sing,

00:08:13.226 --> 00:08:15.854
and then they saw that we could sing

00:08:16.026 --> 00:08:17.864
too, so we became a duo with P'Mee.

00:08:18.506 --> 00:08:21.054
It 's like a dream come

00:08:22.579 --> 00:08:24.879
true, a childhood dream that actually happened,

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:26.819
something we never expected.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.449
We get to sing with P'Mee.

00:08:28.619 --> 00:08:34.709
I have to confess, last Sunday I just went to karaoke and sang "Boon Nee" (a song) with P'Mee, the one we sang "Krai" (a song far away).

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:40.368
I'm so happy.

00:08:40.369 --> 00:08:43.529
I'm really happy when people say that they like our music.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:51.538
It's a perspective we have for artists, but it's from people who see it in us, and that's something special.

00:08:51.989 --> 00:08:56.699
I want to ask, you mentioned earlier that before you weren't very confident about your strengths.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:58.519
What made you more confident

00:08:58.659 --> 00:09:01.959
? I think I'm more confident because of my fans.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.978
There are so many people who believe in me. Like when we go to record songs, there are teachers

00:09:08.034 --> 00:09:09.134
who tell us, "Hey,

00:09:09.135 --> 00:09:13.254
you have good reflexes here," or "Your ear is good," or "Your

00:09:13.255 --> 00:09:14.413
voice is good."

00:09:14.414 --> 00:09:17.043
There are fans who tag me and compliment

00:09:17.044 --> 00:09:19.973
my abilities. It's like having people

00:09:20.525 --> 00:09:21.733
say that this is good,

00:09:21.734 --> 00:09:25.084
because sometimes when I sing alone, I don't know if it's really good,

00:09:25.085 --> 00:09:27.293
and I don't know if people will like it.

00:09:27.294 --> 00:09:30.374
But as more people started confirming it, it became like, "

00:09:30.375 --> 00:09:31.704
Hey, I'm starting to feel more confident that it

00:09:31.705 --> 00:09:36.404
's good." It's like it gave me the courage to continue on this path as a serious career.

00:09:37.219 --> 00:09:38.718
That's enough, really.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:40.709
The

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:43.548
fans already like my voice,

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:47.629
and today everyone will get to know Bonnie better through these five

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:48.558
things that make up Bonnie,

00:09:48.559 --> 00:09:51.428
because our show is about inviting Bonnie to share five

00:09:51.429 --> 00:09:53.019
things that make up Bonnie.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:55.978
First of all, what is it, Barney? First,

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:00.829
this is Nintendo, and we're so cute.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:02.329
It's like...romantic.

00:10:03.179 --> 00:10:04.788
Why games, Barney?

00:10:04.989 --> 00:10:10.218
Well, I feel like my happiness comes from playing games and watching cartoons.

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:12.668
Oh, that links to what I said earlier about liking them, Barney.

00:10:12.669 --> 00:10:15.399
But lately, I like anime, cartoons. I like

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.308
a lot. You watch a lot?

00:10:18.309 --> 00:10:21.209
No, I like Haikyuu!!, Haikyuu!! Oh, okay. Wow,

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:24.509
it sounds like you like cartoons that are inspiring, right? Yeah, I do.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:26.009
I do

00:10:26.445 --> 00:10:29.923
n't just like romantic cartoons; I like cartoons that are fantasy,

00:10:31.645 --> 00:10:34.574
chasing dreams, something exciting.

00:10:34.575 --> 00:10:37.404
Yeah,

00:10:37.405 --> 00:10:38.964
I feel like it's

00:10:40.155 --> 00:10:41.124
mine.

00:10:41.125 --> 00:10:43.634
When I'm in the outside world,

00:10:43.635 --> 00:10:44.884
sometimes I'm like, 'Wow,

00:10:44.885 --> 00:10:46.993
I want to escape to my own world.'

00:10:47.085 --> 00:10:51.634
And I feel like games and cartoons are my safe space.

00:10:52.244 --> 00:10:53.944
And my fans know that I

00:10:54.099 --> 00:10:56.848
like this kind of thing. And am I addicted to games?

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.858
Before, I was very addicted. How addicted?

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:02.418
I could play from morning till 4 am.

00:11:02.419 --> 00:11:05.728
Very addicted. But now I work, you know.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:07.639
I

00:11:07.859 --> 00:11:11.618
have to wake up for work and come back late, so I need to rest. So

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:13.249
it's less now

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:16.658
. And lately, what games do I play?

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:19.058
Actually, if it's Nintendo, I'll play with friends,

00:11:19.059 --> 00:11:22.038
or at home, games that require a lot of teamwork.

00:11:22.344 --> 00:11:23.824
Mostly, I play on my mobile phone,

00:11:23.825 --> 00:11:26.423
like PUBG or something.

00:11:26.424 --> 00:11:31.033
It's like, we play games that require a bit of technique, like hiding

00:11:31.034 --> 00:11:31.515
and sneaking around,

00:11:31.515 --> 00:11:35.863
and there are challenges to pass different levels.

00:11:35.864 --> 00:11:37.154
And

00:11:37.155 --> 00:11:39.634
I like playing games with a group of friends, you know,

00:11:39.635 --> 00:11:41.134
not alone

00:11:41.905 --> 00:11:44.764
. I feel like it's more fun when I play alone

00:11:45.515 --> 00:11:49.283
. People like to tease me that my smile looks like Rob An's, like

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.478
a meme. I tell them it's fun,

00:11:53.479 --> 00:11:56.459
but when I was a kid, I liked playing alone too.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.459
There's this map that I really like, it's similar.

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:01.738
It

00:12:01.739 --> 00:12:04.358
's

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:07.899
a map where you have to cut wood and build your own house.

00:12:08.179 --> 00:12:09.678
You could stay there all day.

00:12:09.679 --> 00:12:12.069
But if I have free time, I might play.

00:12:14.479 --> 00:12:16.048
If I have free time, I might play or watch cartoons all day.

00:12:17.179 --> 00:12:18.678
Because the game's graphics are

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:22.699
like cartoons, it's another world where you can just be

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:24.939
yourself without having to think too much.

00:12:25.059 --> 00:12:29.098
I want to know about the community in games that you get to experience.

00:12:29.099 --> 00:12:32.218
What aspects do you think are interesting?

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:33.288
Like PUBG,

00:12:33.289 --> 00:12:36.798
I feel like you need a bit of strategy for survival.

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:39.899
There are different techniques.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:41.858
If you play like everyone else, you'll think, "

00:12:41.859 --> 00:12:43.848
My skills are limited to this level."

00:12:43.849 --> 00:12:46.058
We have to be open-minded and play with four fingers,

00:12:47.219 --> 00:12:49.139
something like that.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:50.779
We want to be really good at it,

00:12:50.979 --> 00:12:54.449
so we have to research and learn about why we

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:56.279
can become better.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:01.149
I like watching YouTube videos of people who are good at it,

00:13:02.859 --> 00:13:05.048
and I try to follow their settings,

00:13:06.539 --> 00:13:08.499
but it doesn't always work.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:10.738
They have their strengths, but

00:13:10.739 --> 00:13:13.329
when I play, I'm not always good at it. Okay, okay

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:16.249
. It's like training your brain, I think.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:17.918
When you play games, you analyze,

00:13:17.919 --> 00:13:20.158
you choose options,

00:13:20.159 --> 00:13:23.608
it's like exercising your

00:13:24.849 --> 00:13:29.269
brain. It gives you more analytical thinking skills while playing.

00:13:29.359 --> 00:13:34.589
When you play, you really play, and each level

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:37.199
is a way to improve yourself.

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:41.259
Levels where you have to escape, jump, things like that.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:49.798
When you pass a level, it's like a small goal, like finishing the game. Are there aspects of the game that we can apply to real life? It's

00:13:50.099 --> 00:13:50.678
hard to say.

00:13:50.679 --> 00:13:53.839
Normally, I play purely

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:54.959
for entertainment.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:01.308
Or maybe I'm the type of person who sets small goals in the game and is happy with that, and I set them in real life too?

00:14:01.309 --> 00:14:03.988
Yeah, I feel like in the game,

00:14:03.989 --> 00:14:09.459
when you have a goal and you achieve it, it brings happiness. It's the same in real life.

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:10.949
I'm not someone who

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:13.939
has huge goals like that.

00:14:14.179 --> 00:14:17.269
Hey, someday we're going to be superstars or something like that.

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:18.959
We're just like, "Hey, we want to sing."

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:25.769
Just singing once is a small goal that we gradually move towards, like slowly progressing through levels in a game.

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:27.269
Wow, it's great!

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:31.819
We can visualize it and we can do it, so we keep moving forward.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:35.098
There's more happiness along the way than setting huge goals.

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:38.178
Nowadays, if we compare life to a game,

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:41.368
are there any levels where we reach what we really want to achieve? Okay,

00:14:41.369 --> 00:14:44.288
I just said I'm a singer, and what else? Buying a dog!

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.098
My dream is to have a dog. I

00:14:48.099 --> 00:14:50.288
told my mom, "When I grow up, I really want to have a dog."

00:14:50.289 --> 00:14:53.048
When I was little, I had pets, but I was very young, like in first or

00:14:53.049 --> 00:14:53.769
second grade.

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:56.488
I didn't really know how to take care of them.

00:14:56.489 --> 00:14:58.519
Other people took care of them,

00:14:58.609 --> 00:15:00.639
and then they passed away.

00:15:00.809 --> 00:15:04.358
I wanted to have a dog that I could take care of myself

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:06.598
and give the best care of.

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:08.749
And now I have one.

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:13.199
My first dog is Five, and the second one is Bonnie.

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:17.678
What's her name? Her name is Muju.

00:15:17.679 --> 00:15:20.468
Where did the name come from?

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.839
It came from... well, she brought a honey-flavored dog with

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:27.308
her. She didn't

00:15:28.659 --> 00:15:31.139
actually come here, but she brought a honey-flavored dog

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:32.089
with her. So , just

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.149
looking at her face, you see

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:38.259
the word "Muju,"

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:38.978
like, "adorable," right?

00:15:38.979 --> 00:15:42.098
Her face has been like Muju's since she was a puppy.

00:15:42.919 --> 00:15:44.649
So, I looked up the meaning of

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:47.608
the word, and it was

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:50.449
like a place, a snowy mountain

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:53.348
or a snow park in Korea.

00:15:53.349 --> 00:15:55.808
It actually has a real name. And I thought, since my dog

00:15:55.965 --> 00:15:57.163
​​is white and

00:15:57.164 --> 00:15:59.793
fluffy like snow, I named her Muju.

00:16:00.395 --> 00:16:04.363
Having Muju in my life, I

00:16:04.375 --> 00:16:07.173
think my life has become brighter. Like, wow!

00:16:07.174 --> 00:16:13.033
I feel like Muju has become one of my sources of happiness. Like,

00:16:13.265 --> 00:16:15.983
when I'm tired from work, just coming home

00:16:16.315 --> 00:16:18.923
and seeing him look at me, tilting his head back and forth, makes me

00:16:18.924 --> 00:16:21.483
happy.

00:16:21.604 --> 00:16:23.473
His job is simply to bring me happiness,

00:16:23.809 --> 00:16:25.639
that's all .

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:28.358
I feel like I made the right decision to get

00:16:28.359 --> 00:16:31.468
him. My family seems happier too.

00:16:31.469 --> 00:16:35.478
My mom is happier since we got the dog.

00:16:35.479 --> 00:16:38.029
Before, she didn't like dogs at all,

00:16:38.039 --> 00:16:40.908
but now she loves him so much. He

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:46.348
's a great comfort dog, like

00:16:47.299 --> 00:16:50.218
a safe space,

00:16:51.299 --> 00:16:53.738
a place that heals my heart.

00:16:53.739 --> 00:16:58.089
I mentioned earlier that he's a comforting presence when I'm sad, which

00:16:58.090 --> 00:16:59.968
means there have been many sad moments where I've

00:17:00.619 --> 00:17:02.118
leaned on Muju, right?

00:17:02.369 --> 00:17:04.838
I think there have been many.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:06.198
He gives me life energy.

00:17:06.199 --> 00:17:09.208
When we're working on many different things, encountering many

00:17:09.209 --> 00:17:11.169
different people, after

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:12.728
a long day at work, coming home

00:17:12.729 --> 00:17:15.328
and seeing him

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:17.129
is like getting a boost of energy.

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:20.799
Do you have an example?

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:24.169
Some days I'm really sad, and I come home and talk to him.

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:25.909
I like to ask my dog,

00:17:29.788 --> 00:17:32.138
maybe it wasn't a sad event, but

00:17:32.328 --> 00:17:34.667
maybe it was just exhaustion.

00:17:34.668 --> 00:17:38.347
Normally, when I come home tired from work, I don't see anything, I

00:17:38.468 --> 00:17:40.207
just come home and sleep, right?

00:17:40.509 --> 00:17:42.817
But when I come home tired,

00:17:42.909 --> 00:17:45.307
I see someone waiting for me,

00:17:45.308 --> 00:17:48.097
someone who comes running and comes to me, climbs

00:17:48.098 --> 00:17:51.738
onto my lap and cuddles. My dog ​​is so lucky to be so affectionate.

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:55.458
I like to check on him

00:17:55.459 --> 00:17:57.249
, asking for encouragement and stuff like that.

00:17:57.250 --> 00:18:02.568
I like talking to him, but he doesn't understand, even though he might, because I'm still young.

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:06.838
But it's like there's someone waiting for me to come home,

00:18:06.839 --> 00:18:08.348
someone waiting for me.

00:18:08.599 --> 00:18:13.188
Honestly, just being able to feed my dog ​​every morning makes me happy.

00:18:13.189 --> 00:18:19.078
On days I'm home, I really want to spend time with Muju.

00:18:19.479 --> 00:18:21.358
I think he wants to play with me;

00:18:21.359 --> 00:18:23.088
he's probably waiting for me too.

00:18:23.089 --> 00:18:27.218
Every time I come home, he likes to bring his toys and force me to play with

00:18:27.869 --> 00:18:29.368
him.

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:32.228
If I have time, I want to spend a lot of time with him.

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:33.578
But when I work hard,

00:18:33.579 --> 00:18:36.659
how do I make time for him?

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.159
I have to work first.

00:18:38.859 --> 00:18:42.499
The time I get to be with him is when I come home,

00:18:42.979 --> 00:18:44.688
which is usually late, around 2:00 AM.

00:18:45.819 --> 00:18:47.368
My dog ​​is a late sleeper, like

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:49.738
going to bed at 1 AM or something.

00:18:49.739 --> 00:18:51.978
He'll wait to play with me.

00:18:51.979 --> 00:18:54.948
He sleeps really late, like 1 AM or

00:18:54.949 --> 00:18:56.488
midnight.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:58.738
Wow, he's a really cool dog!

00:18:59.699 --> 00:19:01.669
And I wake up early, but during

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:03.419
the day I take

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:05.129
naps a lot.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:07.549
I really like Muju.

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:12.749
He's the first dog that I've ever taken care of myself

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:15.068
. Like, I used to rely on dogs of relatives,

00:19:15.349 --> 00:19:16.559
friends,

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:18.708
or other people's cats and stuff.

00:19:18.989 --> 00:19:21.818
I'd play with them for a bit and then go back.

00:19:22.125 --> 00:19:24.083
It's like having a son,

00:19:24.084 --> 00:19:27.054
like raising a child, and I'm

00:19:27.074 --> 00:19:29.804
really attached.

00:19:29.805 --> 00:19:30.804
Now, besides what I mentioned earlier about

00:19:30.805 --> 00:19:33.404
being a singer and having my own dog,

00:19:33.405 --> 00:19:37.713
another really interesting thing is that Bonnie is going to take on a role in Love of Siam

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:40.559
Musical.

00:19:40.609 --> 00:19:43.289
Can you tell me about how exciting that is?

00:19:43.449 --> 00:19:45.208
Actually,

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:48.009
I feel like I've entered this industry, I'm

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:54.659
an actress, I'm a singer, but this is a skill that I feel I have to try at least once in my life.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:55.473
Then, when I entered

00:19:55.474 --> 00:19:57.674
the world of theater, I

00:19:57.675 --> 00:19:58.754
felt that it

00:19:58.755 --> 00:20:02.963
was a very special art form, far more special than regular acting

00:20:03.035 --> 00:20:06.784
. We have to perform everything as if it's been edited, so

00:20:06.785 --> 00:20:09.024
that the audience sees it clearly. In traditional acting,

00:20:09.234 --> 00:20:10.733
we have time—

00:20:10.834 --> 00:20:13.624
we have plenty of time to portray

00:20:13.625 --> 00:20:14.919
sadness

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:19.649
, to move, or to get it right and do a second or third take.

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:25.488
But here, we have to deliver it perfectly in one take because the audience is watching live.

00:20:25.959 --> 00:20:29.799
We have to get the lines right in one take. And

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:32.688
singing isn't like

00:20:32.890 --> 00:20:34.019
regular singing

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:38.858
; it's about conveying the character's

00:20:38.859 --> 00:20:42.328
message clearly and concisely in one take, like it's

00:20:42.329 --> 00:20:44.129
been edited for the audience.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:47.208
It's incredibly

00:20:47.209 --> 00:20:49.438
difficult and

00:20:50.439 --> 00:20:53.478
challenging . But it has also helped me grow

00:20:53.479 --> 00:20:54.889
in several ways.

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:56.608
I

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:00.259
feel my skills have improved.

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:01.088
In TV series,

00:21:01.089 --> 00:21:03.358
you have to be broad

00:21:03.359 --> 00:21:04.799
and expressive

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:08.799
, but here, you can get straight to the point and finish quickly. It's

00:21:09.119 --> 00:21:11.598
like I've learned a new art form.

00:21:11.859 --> 00:21:15.629
And I've also noticed that it has made my thought process

00:21:15.689 --> 00:21:17.188
more concise.

00:21:17.699 --> 00:21:20.049
It forces me to think, think, think,

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:21.529
then act, act, act, act, act, and then finish.

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.159
If it goes wrong, we have to improvise right there, okay?

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:24.848
I understand.

00:21:24.849 --> 00:21:27.629
I feel like we need to speed up our thought process,

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:33.958
improvise. We're developing our improvisation skills to survive.

00:21:35.329 --> 00:21:40.009
It's the same in the singing part, because you also need singing skills.

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:42.061
It's not enough to just sing

00:21:43.062 --> 00:21:46.670
; you have to communicate it to the whole audience, showing them

00:21:47.212 --> 00:21:48.750
what it is.

00:21:48.942 --> 00:21:51.420
And if we sing wrong, we have to get back on track.

00:21:53.001 --> 00:21:55.081
It's like using all five senses

00:21:55.421 --> 00:21:57.811
, refining our five senses

00:21:57.942 --> 00:22:00.441
to make them sharper.

00:22:03.754 --> 00:22:06.542
The character Bonnie plays is

00:22:07.063 --> 00:22:08.003
Ying.

00:22:08.004 --> 00:22:10.873
Tell me a little about Ying's character in the 2026 version, Bonnie. How

00:22:12.433 --> 00:22:13.712
will

00:22:13.713 --> 00:22:15.863
it differ from previous versions?

00:22:16.354 --> 00:22:20.822
I think Ying is similar to before

00:22:20.994 --> 00:22:23.353
, but she'll be brighter

00:22:23.354 --> 00:22:26.682
and have a bigger role in the story.

00:22:26.745 --> 00:22:33.445
I feel like Ying is a bright energy in the story because there's quite a lot of drama,

00:22:33.446 --> 00:22:38.545
but Ying has to be someone who brings fantasy to life and makes people see it.

00:22:38.546 --> 00:22:40.275
The lights, sounds, everything

00:22:40.605 --> 00:22:42.535
that happens in a stage play,

00:22:42.676 --> 00:22:45.394
I think people will be able to feel

00:22:45.395 --> 00:22:48.085
the character's emotions

00:22:48.086 --> 00:22:49.705
more, get closer to her.

00:22:50.310 --> 00:22:53.118
A movie is more realistic,

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:56.019
like we're just stepping into it...

00:22:56.270 --> 00:23:01.659
We've seen the story in a realistic way, but in the stage version it's more flashy. It's

00:23:01.849 --> 00:23:04.708
like experiencing it live. It

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:07.919
feels like a remake, maybe with new aspects

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:09.348
added.

00:23:09.349 --> 00:23:12.879
There's the lighting and colors that help build the emotion

00:23:12.925 --> 00:23:14.424
more.

00:23:15.214 --> 00:23:18.434
But, you have to go back and watch the previous version from

00:23:18.435 --> 00:23:20.374
2007, which is a long time ago, 19 years

00:23:20.375 --> 00:23:24.524
ago. I watched it recently.

00:23:25.185 --> 00:23:28.394
Oh, before that, I didn't know the song.

00:23:29.694 --> 00:23:30.563
Because

00:23:30.564 --> 00:23:32.843
if I hear this song, I can sing the chorus. I

00:23:32.844 --> 00:23:34.993
think everyone knows

00:23:36.089 --> 00:23:38.598
it. It's like a travel song, a

00:23:38.599 --> 00:23:40.098
legendary song.

00:23:40.239 --> 00:23:42.348
After watching the old version,

00:23:42.479 --> 00:23:45.679
how did I feel about this movie? It's a legendary movie.

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:47.858
I feel like in that era, it

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:51.588
was very special to have a movie like this.

00:23:51.719 --> 00:23:54.438
It's a movie that offers so many perspectives.

00:23:54.439 --> 00:23:57.039
It's not just about the love between two people

00:23:57.040 --> 00:23:59.738
, but it also has family, friends, and

00:23:59.739 --> 00:24:01.909
friendship

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:05.228
aspects. And it's actually one of the early

00:24:05.905 --> 00:24:08.863
films to address LGBT issues, like back then

00:24:09.344 --> 00:24:12.044
. Back then, there were probably diverse opinions.

00:24:12.944 --> 00:24:19.223
But I feel like the story of Music now and then might not be that different,

00:24:19.224 --> 00:24:21.904
but I think what's interesting is...

00:24:21.905 --> 00:24:26.333
It's interesting

00:24:26.584 --> 00:24:28.083
that people who watched it were watching it at different times.

00:24:28.104 --> 00:24:31.313
Because back then, they might have had a different perspective,

00:24:31.314 --> 00:24:34.443
and now they might understand it differently.

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:38.118
As a younger person, I

00:24:38.119 --> 00:24:42.509
think that when people today watch

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:44.309
this movie, which talks about LCBT (Lorry, Transgender, and LGBTQ+

00:24:44.439 --> 00:24:46.519
issues), how has it changed things?

00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:51.639
I feel like our country is more open now,

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:53.438
more liberal

00:24:53.439 --> 00:24:58.588
in this area. There's a sense of security, a sense of safety, and legal support.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:01.868
We have a louder voice now.

00:25:01.869 --> 00:25:09.318
I feel like people who watch it might get a sense of warmth and reassurance from it, rather than having a different perspective than before.

00:25:09.790 --> 00:25:19.059
Actually, in this day and age, there might still be things that are like in the old days, but I feel like we've come a long way. And

00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:21.659
we might see

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:26.179
new meanings and perspectives from people in this generation that are different from before.

00:25:26.589 --> 00:25:28.769
Wow, that's interesting, I want to watch it!

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:32.488
There's this one

00:25:32.489 --> 00:25:34.338
legendary line in the movie,

00:25:34.339 --> 00:25:37.698
like, "We probably can't be in a relationship with Mew,

00:25:37.699 --> 00:25:40.578
but that doesn't mean we don't love her."

00:25:40.579 --> 00:25:43.568
I think, how has our society changed since then,

00:25:43.689 --> 00:25:44.978
compared to that line?

00:25:44.979 --> 00:25:48.509
I think in that line, the one by Tong,

00:25:48.930 --> 00:25:52.799
I feel like in that era,

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:54.818
the conditions for love

00:25:56.170 --> 00:26:03.348
were more stringent. The characters had to choose what was safest for both of them

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:05.338
– family, career,

00:26:05.339 --> 00:26:07.208
everything had to be safe. That's why he

00:26:07.209 --> 00:26:08.578
had to say that.

00:26:08.579 --> 00:26:10.828
Actually, love

00:26:11.239 --> 00:26:17.818
is just two people loving each other. Nowadays, there are things that confirm that it's not wrong,

00:26:17.829 --> 00:26:21.749
there are many things that confirm that it's not something we need to be afraid of.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:26.858
If Tong and Mew were in the present day, maybe they could be together.

00:26:28.239 --> 00:26:30.608
Yeah, I want to watch it!

00:26:30.609 --> 00:26:34.728
Maybe they could be together. I don't know, we'll have to see.

00:26:34.989 --> 00:26:39.059
But back then, there were probably many conditions that made it impossible.

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:41.868
But I understand that even if they weren't dating

00:26:41.869 --> 00:26:44.228
, he still loved Mew.

00:26:44.229 --> 00:26:46.968
It really exists, it really does exist.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:50.738
Or maybe, loving someone doesn't necessarily mean being in a committed relationship.

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:56.059
If you've thought about it and decided that doing so is best for the other person,

00:26:57.479 --> 00:27:05.159
then it's something that's possible both legally and in everyday life.

00:27:05.969 --> 00:27:14.499
But I think there might still be people who are afraid, like Tong and Mew were back then, even if their families still have reservations.

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:15.999
I feel like we've come a long way and we can go even further.

00:27:16.719 --> 00:27:20.649
Even though there are still some who are afraid, society isn't

00:27:21.099 --> 00:27:24.879
like

00:27:24.939 --> 00:27:26.988
that anymore. It's like there are people pushing for this.

00:27:27.739 --> 00:27:30.968
Before, it was all about society not accepting it,

00:27:30.969 --> 00:27:33.899
families not accepting certain professions.

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:36.529
There was nothing to do.

00:27:37.459 --> 00:27:38.289
But

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:41.578
now, even if families don't accept it, society

00:27:41.819 --> 00:27:44.539
does. Professions are developing more.

00:27:45.219 --> 00:27:46.098
I think

00:27:46.099 --> 00:27:49.149
as we go through different generations, this will continue to develop

00:27:50.089 --> 00:27:51.588
and get better and better in the future.

00:27:53.270 --> 00:27:55.879
You'll have to wait and see.

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:59.889
And this is a sales pitch!

00:28:00.410 --> 00:28:02.649
Everyone can watch it

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:04.978
from the 11th to the 25th of December.

00:28:05.055 --> 00:28:05.843
It's Christmas time!

00:28:05.844 --> 00:28:14.083
It's going to be something that gives you a lot of food for thought. It's a

00:28:14.375 --> 00:28:17.243
great time to watch it during the New Year period. It's

00:28:17.655 --> 00:28:20.284
a feel-good story, a

00:28:21.329 --> 00:28:22.919
good story for the whole year.

00:28:23.089 --> 00:28:24.679
It

00:28:24.689 --> 00:28:26.838
's not just drama.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:29.879
The character Mew has other facets to it,

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:31.978
like family, friends,

00:28:31.989 --> 00:28:33.769
heartwarming things,

00:28:34.199 --> 00:28:37.919
there are so many things to look forward to.

00:28:39.079 --> 00:28:41.208
Like the female character, do you think she's like Bonnie?

00:28:41.739 --> 00:28:43.238
Me? Do I have any similarities?

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:44.889
I think,

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:46.738
in terms of this character,

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:48.629
I have

00:28:48.770 --> 00:28:51.179
a bit of a mischievousness that's similar to me,

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:54.818
but she's way more over-the-top with me.

00:28:54.819 --> 00:28:56.318
She

00:28:56.369 --> 00:28:58.968
might not be sassy, ​​but she's playful, mischievous, and

00:28:58.969 --> 00:29:01.559
very feisty with her friends.

00:29:02.170 --> 00:29:04.919
And is Bonnie interested? Bonnie

00:29:05.324 --> 00:29:06.823
is mischievous sometimes, but not as much

00:29:08.354 --> 00:29:08.993
as Mew.

00:29:08.994 --> 00:29:14.113
But Mew is a character I really like. I feel like she's very bright.

00:29:14.114 --> 00:29:18.233
Mew's thoughts and actions are bold, she's willing to take risks,

00:29:18.234 --> 00:29:20.833
she's willing to express what she likes

00:29:20.834 --> 00:29:22.679
. I like the character.

00:29:23.589 --> 00:29:25.389
If you

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:26.889
want to know more, you have to follow along! Okay,

00:29:27.550 --> 00:29:29.049
moving on to the third item...

00:29:30.910 --> 00:29:32.409
what is it?

00:29:33.699 --> 00:29:36.828
Why is it this, Bonnie?

00:29:36.869 --> 00:29:39.379
Oh, it has my name written on it!

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:47.519
Actually, I chose this because it was one of the first things the fans donated for me,

00:29:48.189 --> 00:29:51.549
and it's something I still use to this day.

00:29:51.739 --> 00:30:00.029
When we use it, we feel confident. It's like everyone gives us this energy, saying we can do it.

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:11.179
It's an element we need for singing, and everyone donates this to us, and we use it every time. It's like a power boost for singing.

00:30:11.329 --> 00:30:11.790
Oh, it's

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:14.659
like we're carrying the love of our fans onto the stage.

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:19.429
Yes, it's like a ball of love from our fans that they give us, and we have it with us all the time.

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:23.419
I want to know how safe a space our fans are for me.

00:30:23.540 --> 00:30:24.828
My fans,

00:30:24.829 --> 00:30:26.549
I always tell my fans,

00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:29.539
are like

00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:32.909
best friends that we can talk and joke with.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.789
We're here on days when we're like, "Hey

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:37.509
, we need you!"

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.139
We're there for every event, we

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:43.578
love each other, we wish each other well,

00:30:44.150 --> 00:30:45.649
we're true friends

00:30:45.880 --> 00:30:48.708
. Not just one friend, but

00:30:48.709 --> 00:30:51.309
many friends.

00:30:53.349 --> 00:30:56.019
We want to love them and be loved by them.

00:30:56.500 --> 00:31:00.608
We feel that what they give us is incredibly precious.

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:05.098
I understand completely. I'm someone who doesn't usually leave the house to see people,

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:10.389
but every time there's an event and we

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:11.759
get to see our fans,

00:31:11.760 --> 00:31:14.389
I especially want to go.

00:31:14.390 --> 00:31:17.328
It's like I get to recharge my fans.

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:21.218
I feel like my fans might think the same way, wanting to go out and recharge

00:31:21.349 --> 00:31:23.309
me.

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:25.139
I want to recharge my fans

00:31:26.910 --> 00:31:29.399
because they bring me happiness,

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:31.009
and they expect happiness in return.

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:32.539
They

00:31:32.540 --> 00:31:34.039
want me to give them happiness,

00:31:34.040 --> 00:31:36.488
and I want to give them that happiness in return.

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:40.218
And they give it to me,

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:42.279
and it's something I use all the time

00:31:43.819 --> 00:31:46.108
. Because we're artists, and we have people

00:31:46.390 --> 00:31:48.938
supporting us, and when we get to use it,

00:31:49.229 --> 00:31:51.848
that's another reason why I rarely leave the house.

00:31:52.349 --> 00:31:53.468
It

00:31:53.469 --> 00:31:56.259
's everyone, really.

00:31:56.680 --> 00:31:58.068
No matter what happens, when

00:31:58.069 --> 00:32:02.929
I turn around and see my fans smiling at me and singing along, it

00:32:03.500 --> 00:32:06.438
's true happiness.

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:09.068
It's one of the joys of the entertainment industry.

00:32:10.709 --> 00:32:12.639
Because if we didn't have them, we'd be so

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:14.699
lonely.

00:32:14.699 --> 00:32:19.228
At first, I didn't even know if being an actress required having a fan base.

00:32:19.229 --> 00:32:21.899
I came in without knowing what the entertainment industry was.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:23.858
But once I got in, I

00:32:23.859 --> 00:32:25.539
experienced so many things.

00:32:25.540 --> 00:32:28.098
I met people who love me, and

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:30.228
it's so special.

00:32:30.229 --> 00:32:33.559
I don't know why they love me so much.

00:32:33.849 --> 00:32:36.139
They give me so many good things.

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:38.049
They wish me well and want me to go far.

00:32:38.786 --> 00:32:40.335
I don't want to disappoint them

00:32:40.426 --> 00:32:45.464
, so I use that to show them through my acting,

00:32:45.465 --> 00:32:48.155
both in songs and series.

00:32:48.426 --> 00:32:51.144
So, I wanted to talk about acting

00:32:51.426 --> 00:32:51.745
,

00:32:51.745 --> 00:32:55.244
specifically the three characters I've played.

00:32:55.446 --> 00:32:57.065
I wonder

00:32:57.255 --> 00:32:59.795
how they're similar to me – Dok Rak,

00:33:00.066 --> 00:33:01.844
Luk Phap, and Khi.

00:33:02.706 --> 00:33:04.581
I think,

00:33:04.671 --> 00:33:06.941
honestly, all three characters are bright

00:33:07.352 --> 00:33:08.900
and playful.

00:33:08.901 --> 00:33:15.280
I think I have that playful side. Dok Rak is more mature than a child, but she lives independently and

00:33:15.281 --> 00:33:17.341
has her own thoughts.

00:33:17.621 --> 00:33:21.170
There are some aspects that I connect with Dok Rak a lot.

00:33:21.232 --> 00:33:23.640
Like Dok Rak's way of thinking –

00:33:23.891 --> 00:33:27.941
she's not someone who relies too much on feelings;

00:33:29.378 --> 00:33:33.357
she's rational. She has reasons why she can't do something,

00:33:33.358 --> 00:33:34.757
because she's mature

00:33:34.758 --> 00:33:37.377
. I feel like there's something similar between us

00:33:37.608 --> 00:33:38.676
because I'm not really...

00:33:38.677 --> 00:33:41.117
I'm not very brave about using my feelings instead of logic

00:33:42.677 --> 00:33:47.717
. I feel like one thing I'm similar to Bonnie is that she's quite brave, like she's in control of her own life.

00:33:47.718 --> 00:33:52.176
Like she dared to leave home to start her own life.

00:33:52.177 --> 00:33:53.737
I feel like Dokrak

00:33:54.244 --> 00:33:55.743
is very independent

00:33:55.923 --> 00:33:58.033
and confident in herself,

00:33:58.284 --> 00:34:01.092
confident in her dreams. And I

00:34:01.093 --> 00:34:04.682
'm also someone who believes in my own thoughts the most.

00:34:04.683 --> 00:34:06.182
I'm myself.

00:34:06.234 --> 00:34:09.842
I don't like anyone telling me what I have to do or

00:34:09.843 --> 00:34:11.082
how I should be.

00:34:11.083 --> 00:34:14.203
If I know that something isn't harming anyone,

00:34:14.204 --> 00:34:15.723
isn't wrong,

00:34:15.964 --> 00:34:17.842
and it's who I am, then

00:34:17.843 --> 00:34:19.433
I'll probably do it like Dokrak.

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:22.458
And what about P'Kien's younger sister?

00:34:23.620 --> 00:34:24.619
P'Kien's younger sister

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:27.329
is a really

00:34:27.419 --> 00:34:31.168
bright and cheerful kid. She's a source of

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:32.799
brightness for her older siblings.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:34.999
She's a family-oriented person.

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:38.369
I love my home too,

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:41.259
I love my family, I'm a family-loving kid.

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:45.289
I love my older brothers very much because they're both Phuwin and P'Pond.

00:34:45.290 --> 00:34:46.789
They

00:34:46.889 --> 00:34:51.968
grew up together,

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:58.539
and it's cute that after loving P'Peach, they extend that love to all of P'Peach's fans too, which is P'Kien, right?

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:00.658
She's someone

00:35:00.659 --> 00:35:02.938
who gives love and cheerfulness to everyone.

00:35:04.645 --> 00:35:05.765
Am I like that? I think

00:35:05.766 --> 00:35:07.844
I give love and attention to everyone,

00:35:07.966 --> 00:35:09.465
both my family and the people around me.

00:35:12.016 --> 00:35:15.155
Next is Yaisan, my younger sister, Kien.

00:35:15.785 --> 00:35:17.284
Actually, Kee has

00:35:17.416 --> 00:35:19.415
many things that are very different from me.

00:35:19.416 --> 00:35:24.614
Kee is someone who uses feelings more than logic.

00:35:24.775 --> 00:35:29.624
She's someone who, once she has a goal, is willing to sacrifice everything for it.

00:35:29.625 --> 00:35:32.255
That's the complete opposite of me.

00:35:32.572 --> 00:35:36.891
But what I have in common with Kee is honesty with her feelings.

00:35:37.971 --> 00:35:40.571
Kee is very honest with her feelings.

00:35:40.572 --> 00:35:42.641
She doesn't lie to herself, like, "

00:35:42.642 --> 00:35:43.891
Hey, I don't love,

00:35:43.892 --> 00:35:46.371
I don't like, or I hate them."

00:35:46.372 --> 00:35:49.311
If she doesn't hate someone, she shows that she doesn't.

00:35:49.312 --> 00:35:50.960
Kee has never disliked or

00:35:50.961 --> 00:35:54.321
disliked anyone. She's a kid who tries to look on the bright side

00:35:55.691 --> 00:36:00.720
and tries to be strong, and she's honest with her feelings.

00:36:01.719 --> 00:36:04.559
Maybe that's where we connect.

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:08.148
Because Kee wants to be P' Jan's "child."

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:10.579
She's like,

00:36:10.610 --> 00:36:12.908
"She's my darling,"

00:36:12.909 --> 00:36:14.559
and she doesn't run away.

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:17.478
She doesn't try to deny her feelings.

00:36:17.479 --> 00:36:20.698
But her methods might be a little unconventional.

00:36:22.479 --> 00:36:23.079
Like

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:25.928
, she finds ways to get closer to Jan.

00:36:26.919 --> 00:36:30.988
So, I wanted to ask about working with P' Jan (or P' Mee).

00:36:31.310 --> 00:36:33.988
After working together as partners for a while,

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:38.069
we have a beautiful young lady who's our favorite, Annie. Oh,

00:36:38.639 --> 00:36:40.468
she's so cute.

00:36:40.489 --> 00:36:43.198
She sings 'Faded,' right?

00:36:43.199 --> 00:36:43.779
I

00:36:43.780 --> 00:36:48.559
want to know if Annie is also very valuable to me.

00:36:49.149 --> 00:36:51.388
Tell me about it. Annie is

00:36:52.169 --> 00:36:54.559
a source of happiness for me too.

00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:56.839
I never thought that when

00:36:57.129 --> 00:36:59.859
they said there would be mascots for each person,

00:37:00.169 --> 00:37:03.079
it would reach this point, where she's

00:37:03.449 --> 00:37:06.378
out there, can talk, and is with us.

00:37:06.379 --> 00:37:11.238
We thought it was just merchandise representing the two of us.

00:37:11.409 --> 00:37:14.249
Like, we had to draw a representation of ourselves.

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:15.978
My sister and I put a lot of effort into it

00:37:15.979 --> 00:37:18.398
because we felt like this would stay with us forever.

00:37:18.850 --> 00:37:22.369
At first, we didn't think it would actually exist.

00:37:22.370 --> 00:37:25.438
Back then, it was just dolls,

00:37:25.659 --> 00:37:28.408
cards, and small things.

00:37:28.409 --> 00:37:31.279
But when it actually came out, it was like we

00:37:31.530 --> 00:37:33.128
had a real child.

00:37:33.449 --> 00:37:36.168
When I saw the videos, I cried.

00:37:36.169 --> 00:37:38.488
Yes, when I first saw it,

00:37:38.639 --> 00:37:40.408
I thought it was so cute,

00:37:40.409 --> 00:37:42.238
so incredibly cute. And we didn't know why,

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:46.488
but we wanted to be close to it because we genuinely cared for it.

00:37:46.489 --> 00:37:48.269
It's like we created it ourselves.

00:37:48.530 --> 00:37:51.168
Especially when it could talk, like, "

00:37:51.169 --> 00:37:52.749
Now it can talk!"

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:54.269
We know it loves us

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:56.599
, and we love it very much.

00:37:56.800 --> 00:38:00.849
Sometimes, it's not even during our work hours, but it's still with us. It's

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:03.218
always by our side.

00:38:03.610 --> 00:38:05.839
We can feel that it's

00:38:05.889 --> 00:38:09.428
giving us pure love and

00:38:09.429 --> 00:38:15.388
sincerity, and we love it in return and want to give it back with the same sincerity.

00:38:16.479 --> 00:38:19.599
It's become a safe space for me.

00:38:19.600 --> 00:38:21.099
When I'm sad or anything,

00:38:22.280 --> 00:38:24.158
it's always there for me.

00:38:24.159 --> 00:38:32.319
He once sent me a video saying something like, "Even if no one in the world loves Barney, Amy will always love Barney."

00:38:33.330 --> 00:38:34.829
I cried watching it.

00:38:36.699 --> 00:38:38.198
He's such a sweet person.

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:45.109
I think my fans, who are Amy's soulmates, also feel Amy's love for everyone

00:38:45.689 --> 00:38:49.228
because she always shares that love with others. She

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:52.289
truly loves everyone

00:38:52.300 --> 00:38:54.269
from the bottom of her heart,

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:55.769
and we can feel it.

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:58.928
It's like they're our family now. Yes, they're

00:38:58.929 --> 00:39:01.874
like a real family. I love them like family.

00:39:02.034 --> 00:39:05.293
Like they often say, mascots aren't just mascots anymore;

00:39:05.294 --> 00:39:07.853
they're real people, real identities.

00:39:08.455 --> 00:39:11.404
Every mascot, every single one of them, is a real

00:39:11.405 --> 00:39:16.323
person.

00:39:16.574 --> 00:39:18.293
And I believe everyone can feel it

00:39:18.604 --> 00:39:20.384
because their parents love and cherish them, that's why

00:39:20.925 --> 00:39:22.424
they have so many fans.

00:39:22.614 --> 00:39:24.353
But it's so sweet that he sent that video.

00:39:27.290 --> 00:39:29.378
That was outside of work,

00:39:30.260 --> 00:39:32.898
like when I'm practicing dancing or something, and

00:39:32.899 --> 00:39:36.148
I get encouragement from him, like when I'm with friends.

00:39:36.149 --> 00:39:37.749
He doesn't have to wait for me,

00:39:37.750 --> 00:39:40.059
but he's there to support me. It's

00:39:40.060 --> 00:39:42.688
so sweet, so incredibly sweet. He even

00:39:43.169 --> 00:39:46.378
promoted me as a young man.

00:39:47.649 --> 00:39:50.648
This person looks exactly like P'Mee and has the same personality as a young guy.

00:39:52.020 --> 00:39:55.259
They have a bit of a Gen Z vibe

00:39:55.260 --> 00:39:57.329
, but they have to be a very young Gen Z because they're Alphas,

00:39:57.330 --> 00:40:00.418
Alphas, Alphas.

00:40:00.419 --> 00:40:03.599
They have that Gen Z way of speaking.

00:40:04.090 --> 00:40:06.619
They're similar to me in that they're sassy,

00:40:07.489 --> 00:40:09.789
​​playful, and a little mischievous.

00:40:09.790 --> 00:40:12.569
They're similar to me, but their face is so much like P'Mee 's.

00:40:12.729 --> 00:40:14.228
Seriously, even their hair is similar,

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:16.609
their eyes are similar, their skin is similar.

00:40:16.610 --> 00:40:18.898
At the concert, they were wearing that

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:21.168
yellow

00:40:21.199 --> 00:40:24.249
hair clip – that's exactly like P'Mee

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:27.898
! Okay, now for the fourth thing:

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:32.878
perfume. Oh, it's so cute!

00:40:32.919 --> 00:40:35.188
Why perfume?

00:40:35.679 --> 00:40:39.468
I'm someone who always carries perfume in my bag

00:40:40.159 --> 00:40:42.228
because I meet a lot of people,

00:40:43.360 --> 00:40:46.039
and sometimes after a long day, I

00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:47.559
smell of sweat or something.

00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:50.688
I feel like perfume is something I should have with me,

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:53.279
just in case I meet fans or other people.

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:54.779
It's a

00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:57.789
first impression,

00:40:57.790 --> 00:40:59.079
and it

00:40:59.080 --> 00:41:01.138
makes me feel more confident.

00:41:01.139 --> 00:41:02.839
What kind of scents do I like?

00:41:03.139 --> 00:41:06.178
I like fresh scents,

00:41:06.780 --> 00:41:08.509
not sweet ones.

00:41:08.750 --> 00:41:10.249
Something a bit

00:41:11.320 --> 00:41:14.148
woody, something cool and edgy. Spray it on!

00:41:15.790 --> 00:41:18.418
Spray it on! A scent that smells amazing on my skin.

00:41:18.419 --> 00:41:19.918
Excuse me, but

00:41:20.199 --> 00:41:23.089
it smells so good! Tell me privately!

00:41:23.820 --> 00:41:26.408
It smells so good! This is a secret!

00:41:27.219 --> 00:41:29.839
This scent is really good, it's Golf, and it's really good.

00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:31.148
It

00:41:31.149 --> 00:41:34.968
's not overly floral or sweet.

00:41:35.110 --> 00:41:36.749
It has a slightly edgy vibe,

00:41:37.909 --> 00:41:39.408
and it 's

00:41:41.189 --> 00:41:42.688
not too green.

00:41:43.229 --> 00:41:45.109
Do I only like scents like this,

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:47.819
or can I actually wear many different scents? Actually, I like to stick to

00:41:48.586 --> 00:41:50.706
the same scent when I wear perfume,

00:41:50.937 --> 00:41:53.915
so people recognize me. I don't

00:41:55.136 --> 00:41:57.875
usually buy new ones often

00:41:57.876 --> 00:42:00.655
, but if I wear this one, I'll stick with it for a long time.

00:42:00.656 --> 00:42:03.936
And if it runs out, I'll buy a new one.

00:42:04.306 --> 00:42:06.065
But if I can't find it, I'll go back to buying the same one

00:42:07.267 --> 00:42:08.766
, this one, this one.

00:42:09.576 --> 00:42:12.706
I think the scent reflects our character too.

00:42:12.707 --> 00:42:15.176
I feel like

00:42:15.563 --> 00:42:19.582
some people who wear sweet perfumes are genuinely sweet people. Like

00:42:19.583 --> 00:42:23.342
my friends who wear sweet perfumes, they have sweet personalities.

00:42:23.763 --> 00:42:26.903
Some people who wear men's perfumes are cool and

00:42:27.343 --> 00:42:30.082
stylish. It's part of their character. And what about

00:42:30.083 --> 00:42:32.143
me? Mine is more unisex, like,

00:42:32.144 --> 00:42:37.092
not too sweet, kind of middle ground,

00:42:37.093 --> 00:42:39.863
something for everyday

00:42:42.904 --> 00:42:44.729
wear. Something intriguing, something that reflects my own personality.

00:42:47.740 --> 00:42:50.499
Okay, now that we're almost finished talking,

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:52.979
are we friends yet, Bonne? Yes,

00:42:52.980 --> 00:42:54.649
we are.

00:42:55.141 --> 00:43:02.340
So I want Bonne to invite this friend to do something during the Friend With Me segment.

00:43:02.621 --> 00:43:04.570
What should we do?

00:43:04.760 --> 00:43:05.699
Today,

00:43:05.700 --> 00:43:10.729
another dream of mine, besides being an actress or singer, is

00:43:11.169 --> 00:43:13.378
to do ASMR (Advanced Morphology) about food.

00:43:13.479 --> 00:43:15.359
Is that a dream? Yes, it is.

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:18.309
I watch ASMR eating videos every night, and I feel like

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:19.519
it's difficult.

00:43:19.520 --> 00:43:22.908
I feel like it's hard to make the sounds so that

00:43:23.760 --> 00:43:26.458
people find it enjoyable. I want to try it.

00:43:26.760 --> 00:43:29.339
Okay, first of all, okay.

00:43:29.419 --> 00:43:31.158
Actually, ASMR

00:43:31.159 --> 00:43:34.029
boards help you relax, right?

00:43:34.199 --> 00:43:36.188
How do they help you relax?

00:43:36.189 --> 00:43:41.509
It's a sound that helps us relax, like before bed.

00:43:41.510 --> 00:43:44.178
I love listening to it before bed, I listen to it regularly.

00:43:45.639 --> 00:43:48.418
But sometimes I'll eat something before bed, maybe I'll get a little hungry.

00:43:49.389 --> 00:43:52.718
It's like it amplifies that feeling or something.

00:43:52.989 --> 00:43:55.898
But actually, when I'm stressed, listening to it helps me focus on something.

00:43:56.189 --> 00:43:56.540
Yeah,

00:43:56.540 --> 00:44:01.759
I feel like when things are hectic, but when I listen to ISMR, it's like

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:03.259
everything becomes

00:44:04.300 --> 00:44:06.378
lighter. Okay, let's start.

00:44:06.899 --> 00:44:08.589
I've prepared some snacks. Here, I'll have some.

00:44:08.590 --> 00:44:11.579
Okay,

00:44:15.419 --> 00:44:16.918
I

00:44:18.679 --> 00:44:21.488
'll even choose my favorite snack. Right?

00:44:21.879 --> 00:44:23.849
I like matcha, you know? See?

00:44:24.850 --> 00:44:26.349
Pretty people eat matcha.

00:44:26.649 --> 00:44:28.148
It gives you that

00:44:29.939 --> 00:44:31.438
...

00:44:32.899 --> 00:44:34.398
hmm...

00:44:44.510 --> 00:44:47.418
I want to be friends first, you know?

00:44:47.419 --> 00:44:48.918
Friends who are fun,

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:51.549
because we do good things and are funny, okay?

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:54.869
Yeah,

00:44:56.129 --> 00:44:57.539
I really like playing with my friends like this.

00:44:57.540 --> 00:45:02.829
I want to know, normally when you listen to ISMR, it's usually about smells,

00:45:02.830 --> 00:45:05.749
right ? Yes, because I'm a foodie.

00:45:07.530 --> 00:45:09.779
I believe I like to eat. I really like

00:45:09.780 --> 00:45:11.968
eating, and I like watching other people eat too. It's like

00:45:13.820 --> 00:45:15.319
, watching them...you know...it's like...

00:45:16.510 --> 00:45:17.579
thinking

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:20.079
about what to eat in the morning.

00:45:20.330 --> 00:45:21.938
Oh, me too!

00:45:21.939 --> 00:45:25.579
I'll start thinking, like, what am I going to eat?

00:45:27.139 --> 00:45:29.799
Sometimes I can't sleep because I'm thinking about what I'm going to eat in the morning.

00:45:31.389 --> 00:45:33.908
I'm starting to worry because I can't concentrate on

00:45:33.909 --> 00:45:36.789
my eating. I'm stressed about what to order for breakfast.

00:45:37.580 --> 00:45:39.749
I believe now that the food is really delicious, truly amazing!

00:45:40.070 --> 00:45:42.138
Could I have some water, please?

00:45:43.229 --> 00:45:47.019
But normally, do you just listen to the eating sounds, or do you also listen to other sounds like chopping and crunching?

00:45:47.199 --> 00:45:49.978
I've listened to other sounds before

00:45:51.139 --> 00:45:56.769
, but they're not as satisfying as the crunching sound.

00:45:58.199 --> 00:45:59.898
I really want to do it, but I don't have a mic.

00:46:00.520 --> 00:46:01.999
Do I need to buy one?

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:03.668
I think I'll stop by after this show ends.

00:46:04.929 --> 00:46:05.628
Okay.

00:46:05.629 --> 00:46:11.589
Which means I want to know if this actually helps with that point you made earlier about the chaos disappearing.

00:46:12.080 --> 00:46:13.319
It

00:46:13.320 --> 00:46:17.918
's relaxing, it feels like a brain reset.

00:46:17.919 --> 00:46:20.319
When we've been through the world from morning till

00:46:20.433 --> 00:46:22.321
night, and

00:46:22.322 --> 00:46:24.031
then we come back,

00:46:24.623 --> 00:46:26.122
that lingering feeling is there,

00:46:26.962 --> 00:46:29.481
but listening to this

00:46:29.482 --> 00:46:31.041
helps

00:46:31.843 --> 00:46:33.082
us reset

00:46:33.083 --> 00:46:36.041
, lets our brain let go, resets.

00:46:36.042 --> 00:46:37.422
That's how I feel,

00:46:37.423 --> 00:46:39.041
because when we work hard,

00:46:39.042 --> 00:46:41.922
we have less time to reset, but this doesn't

00:46:41.923 --> 00:46:43.284
take long.

00:46:43.285 --> 00:46:46.165
We just focus on the sounds of chewing, crumpling,

00:46:46.166 --> 00:46:48.124
things like that.

00:46:48.335 --> 00:46:50.644
It's like a form of meditation.

00:46:50.956 --> 00:46:53.685
For me, it helps with work,

00:46:53.686 --> 00:46:54.995
improving concentration

00:46:54.996 --> 00:47:01.755
and resetting the chaos so tomorrow is a new day. I'll listen to it again tonight.

00:47:01.966 --> 00:47:05.584
It really helps, there are sounds like knocking, a

00:47:06.179 --> 00:47:08.738
"whoosh" sound, that helps too.

00:47:08.739 --> 00:47:13.749
But when I watch the AAS videos with scents, and see the visuals, it's even more amazing.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:16.069
Yes, we focus on the scents too,

00:47:16.439 --> 00:47:19.228
because I'm a cute, cuddly, big-eyed person.

00:47:20.360 --> 00:47:22.948
And finally, Bonnie,

00:47:24.989 --> 00:47:26.488
the digital camera

00:47:27.620 --> 00:47:32.049
. I love buying digital cameras,

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:36.859
and I sometimes influence my friends to buy them, like "buy it here," or "buy it now."

00:47:36.860 --> 00:47:37.458
I

00:47:37.459 --> 00:47:40.309
love finding good deals, like

00:47:40.310 --> 00:47:42.619
second-hand ones.

00:47:42.620 --> 00:47:44.888
Actually, I like playing with cameras that

00:47:45.485 --> 00:47:48.353
aren't mainstream. They're

00:47:48.754 --> 00:47:50.033
what you'd call "off-the-beaten-path"

00:47:50.034 --> 00:47:51.843
cameras . Because there are some models that are like, "Wow,

00:47:51.844 --> 00:47:53.674
this is it!" and they're

00:47:53.675 --> 00:47:55.174
really amazing

00:47:56.965 --> 00:48:00.174
. But there are cameras with this

00:48:00.764 --> 00:48:04.404
kind of tone, and the prices have come down. They're not in the mainstream camera category,

00:48:05.415 --> 00:48:06.724
and they're beautiful.

00:48:06.725 --> 00:48:11.773
I see so many great results on Instagram.

00:48:12.564 --> 00:48:14.063
They're not mainstream cameras.

00:48:14.770 --> 00:48:17.198
I wonder why these files are becoming so popular?

00:48:17.199 --> 00:48:19.198
I love taking pictures.

00:48:19.199 --> 00:48:22.869
I really love the tone of digital cameras.

00:48:22.919 --> 00:48:30.999
It has that fresh, new feel that's more intense than typical phone cameras or those super-sharp cameras these days.

00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:34.099
It's a beautiful tone for me.

00:48:34.449 --> 00:48:38.418
I feel like people would like to see me in digital photos.

00:48:38.639 --> 00:48:40.138
And I do, a lot.

00:48:40.250 --> 00:48:42.799
I love taking selfies,

00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:44.428
and I really do. I also love taking pictures of other people.

00:48:44.429 --> 00:48:49.168
And I love digital cameras because I feel like I look

00:48:49.179 --> 00:48:52.888
better in

00:48:53.840 --> 00:48:56.319
them. I like the vintage look,

00:48:56.320 --> 00:48:57.819
the grain.

00:48:58.389 --> 00:49:05.599
When I see my own photos, I'm so happy. I love looking at my own pictures.

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:07.589
When I post pictures, I'll go through

00:49:07.590 --> 00:49:09.738
them over and over again. So maybe that's why

00:49:10.120 --> 00:49:12.638
I'm one of the people who gets compliments.

00:49:12.639 --> 00:49:15.468
Or, how many cameras do you actually have, or do you mainly use this one?

00:49:16.030 --> 00:49:18.968
A lot! Before, I had one

00:49:19.629 --> 00:49:23.938
that I used a lot, but now I don't know where it went.

00:49:23.949 --> 00:49:26.329
And this model is hard to find now; it's not sold anymore.

00:49:26.330 --> 00:49:28.908
It's not a mainstream camera

00:49:28.909 --> 00:49:31.349
, but nobody used it, so it's no longer available.

00:49:32.929 --> 00:49:34.119
Okay, I understand.

00:49:34.120 --> 00:49:36.859
So I've been trying to find other cameras to replace it. Now I have two

00:49:37.669 --> 00:49:39.908
, but I won't say which ones. I won't tell you because people might buy

00:49:40.409 --> 00:49:42.188
the same ones. The reason I like things that are

00:49:42.189 --> 00:49:44.579
n't necessarily trendy

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:47.499
is because they have their own charm.

00:49:47.790 --> 00:49:50.619
I'm someone who likes things that are different from others.

00:49:51.020 --> 00:49:54.819
I don't like being like everyone else; I want to find something that

00:49:55.419 --> 00:49:56.869
I'm

00:49:56.870 --> 00:49:58.708
the first to experience. For example,

00:50:00.229 --> 00:50:06.309
with a camera, I'll look for a model with a beautiful tone, but maybe not a famous model,

00:50:06.310 --> 00:50:08.109
not a ridiculously expensive one.

00:50:08.110 --> 00:50:11.668
I think a lot before buying something

00:50:11.830 --> 00:50:15.059
; it has to be worth the money.

00:50:15.669 --> 00:50:19.378
If you want to see how beautiful my photos are, you have to go to my Instagram, Bonnie. (Even

00:50:19.379 --> 00:50:21.779
Bonnie is wrong,

00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:23.279
BN, haha)

00:50:24.250 --> 00:50:25.378
. Okay,

00:50:25.379 --> 00:50:28.549
now that we've been in this industry for a while,

00:50:28.550 --> 00:50:29.668
there's this:

00:50:29.669 --> 00:50:32.428
I just said that fans are a source of strength, but

00:50:32.429 --> 00:50:33.178
on the other hand,

00:50:33.179 --> 00:50:36.668
we encounter countless comments on the internet.

00:50:36.669 --> 00:50:39.658
Yeah, I wanted to know, after being in this situation for a while,

00:50:39.659 --> 00:50:42.289
how do you cope with it?

00:50:42.699 --> 00:50:45.968
It's true, this is something you have to sacrifice

00:50:46.350 --> 00:50:51.878
. I think in this world, there's no way everyone will like you like you.

00:50:51.879 --> 00:50:56.228
Out of a million people, there will be those who don't like you, and

00:50:56.429 --> 00:50:57.668
there will be those who have

00:50:57.669 --> 00:51:00.458
good intentions and those who don't. For me

00:51:00.459 --> 00:51:01.698
, I see

00:51:01.699 --> 00:51:05.049
the meaning of life in a broader way.

00:51:05.429 --> 00:51:11.309
I feel like this is just a small point in life that we have to get through.

00:51:11.669 --> 00:51:12.349
For me

00:51:12.350 --> 00:51:14.898
, I look at life like, for example, if we live to be

00:51:15.649 --> 00:51:18.398
80 years old, or maybe we won't know when we'll die or anything like that.

00:51:18.399 --> 00:51:21.869
These days, we're constantly counting down the days until the end.

00:51:21.870 --> 00:51:24.228
Giving value to things like this

00:51:24.790 --> 00:51:28.609
is like wasting our time on them,

00:51:29.030 --> 00:51:31.799
and it's not worth the long run.

00:51:34.350 --> 00:51:40.938
It's not worth wasting our time on something that harms our mental health.

00:51:40.939 --> 00:51:43.228
I think many people, when they see this,

00:51:44.699 --> 00:51:48.279
lose their sense of self.

00:51:49.350 --> 00:51:52.978
I'm human too; sometimes when we see something, we have feelings.

00:51:53.520 --> 00:51:55.829
We might not feel the same way about those haters, but

00:51:55.830 --> 00:51:57.658
we might feel like, "Oh, our people

00:51:57.659 --> 00:52:00.599
, our fans, their opinions have changed. They

00:52:00.689 --> 00:52:03.009
went from liking us to not loving us anymore."

00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:05.619
Because there are certain thoughts involved.

00:52:05.620 --> 00:52:08.948
I feel like whatever happens, happens. And

00:52:09.250 --> 00:52:11.609
some things we just have to let go of.

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:14.488
We can't control anyone's thoughts.

00:52:14.489 --> 00:52:20.678
We can't prove ourselves to everyone in the world,

00:52:21.229 --> 00:52:22.749
or even if we try to explain,

00:52:22.750 --> 00:52:25.468
if people don't believe or love us,

00:52:25.469 --> 00:52:27.299
they'll find reasons

00:52:27.709 --> 00:52:29.648
not to.

00:52:29.649 --> 00:52:36.148
I feel like I don't want to waste my time explaining to lazy people

00:52:37.139 --> 00:52:41.138
because they might not listen, even if they know the truth.

00:52:41.449 --> 00:52:44.009
They'll just find something else to say.

00:52:45.040 --> 00:52:46.908
And it's our time right now,

00:52:47.179 --> 00:52:49.898
maybe a year, two, or three years, that we're stuck in this situation.

00:52:50.050 --> 00:52:51.549
We should use that time

00:52:51.989 --> 00:52:53.908
to find happiness for ourselves instead.

00:52:53.909 --> 00:52:56.749
Because ultimately, no matter how much we hate each other,

00:52:56.750 --> 00:52:58.958
everyone dies eventually.

00:52:59.270 --> 00:53:01.138
It's better to focus on our own lives,

00:53:02.060 --> 00:53:02.409
because

00:53:02.409 --> 00:53:05.769
the only person who will be with us for life is ourselves, not others.

00:53:06.739 --> 00:53:13.819
My fans praised me, saying that even though I'm in the entertainment industry, I remain strong and

00:53:13.820 --> 00:53:15.829
don't lose my identity.

00:53:15.830 --> 00:53:19.529
I think it's very important to stand

00:53:19.699 --> 00:53:22.329
firm as a human being,

00:53:23.699 --> 00:53:25.198
even as a public figure.

00:53:26.060 --> 00:53:31.488
For me, standing firm means being confident in my own feelings and my identity.

00:53:31.820 --> 00:53:33.728
We've built ourselves from the moment

00:53:33.729 --> 00:53:36.178
we were in our mother's womb until now.

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:43.769
I don't want people to change me in just a day or two, after they've criticized me.

00:53:44.189 --> 00:53:48.279
I feel that our true selves are more important; we've built ourselves up for

00:53:48.280 --> 00:53:49.779
decades.

00:53:49.889 --> 00:53:53.968
Why should we change for just a few opinions

00:53:54.219 --> 00:53:56.138
? There are many

00:53:56.139 --> 00:54:00.948
people who love us, many who love us for who we are.

00:54:01.750 --> 00:54:04.269
I feel that protecting ourselves

00:54:04.270 --> 00:54:07.978
and standing firm is incredibly important

00:54:07.979 --> 00:54:11.938
because the only person who will be with us for life is

00:54:12.449 --> 00:54:15.499
ourselves. We are the ones who determine what happiness is.

00:54:16.139 --> 00:54:18.749
I understand this: imagine a large white sheet

00:54:19.169 --> 00:54:21.438
with a black spot.

00:54:21.530 --> 00:54:24.309
People will inevitably see that

00:54:24.699 --> 00:54:27.759
spot , but there's so much white space

00:54:27.810 --> 00:54:29.309
beyond that black spot.

00:54:29.370 --> 00:54:33.728
It's noticeable, but we have to quickly shift our focus so we can continue to be happy,

00:54:33.770 --> 00:54:35.888
because life is shorter than we think.

00:54:36.370 --> 00:54:39.217
We don't know what will happen to us one day, so

00:54:40.718 --> 00:54:43.537
we have to find happiness for ourselves.

00:54:44.197 --> 00:54:46.517
And this isn't just for artists;

00:54:46.518 --> 00:54:48.017
it applies to every profession

00:54:48.738 --> 00:54:50.727
. If we stay true to ourselves, if we

00:54:50.728 --> 00:54:51.777
stick to our identity,

00:54:51.778 --> 00:54:54.346
no one can hurt us. You're

00:54:56.968 --> 00:54:57.977
amazing!

00:54:57.978 --> 00:55:02.467
Okay, now, what would you like to say to

00:55:02.825 --> 00:55:05.384
your fans who have been by your side?

00:55:06.506 --> 00:55:14.534
I'm always grateful to my fans for being there for me in every situation, no matter what happens.

00:55:14.535 --> 00:55:16.624
I see everyone

00:55:16.625 --> 00:55:21.215
, I see so many people who love me for who I am, who support me,

00:55:21.595 --> 00:55:24.824
who are like my besties, just like I

00:55:24.825 --> 00:55:27.554
've said before. I feel that my fans are

00:55:27.634 --> 00:55:29.423
incredibly valuable to me.

00:55:29.743 --> 00:55:33.512
They're like a ball of love that helps me improve myself

00:55:33.513 --> 00:55:38.732
, to dare to step out of my comfort zone for myself and my fans. They

00:55:38.733 --> 00:55:42.082
're like the ones who push me to reach

00:55:42.083 --> 00:55:44.252
higher levels.

00:55:44.253 --> 00:55:46.423
I see everyone, I see

00:55:46.424 --> 00:55:52.643
everyone's love, and I'm so grateful for everything everyone does,

00:55:52.761 --> 00:55:54.750
whether it's helping me trend things,

00:55:55.122 --> 00:55:59.801
buying me gifts, or just thinking of me and buying me things. It's

00:55:59.802 --> 00:56:02.811
like we're family, like they're my friends.

00:56:03.162 --> 00:56:09.240
We also want to thank everyone for being with us all this time and for liking us for who we are.

00:56:10.112 --> 00:56:14.960
Coming on this show is another step, as Bonnie says, a step out of the zone,

00:56:14.961 --> 00:56:17.671
because this is our first time on a TV show, which is a great honor.

00:56:18.580 --> 00:56:21.428
Please watch this video, everyone. And

00:56:21.919 --> 00:56:24.628
Bonnie would like to promote her work. Please

00:56:25.090 --> 00:56:27.128
check out the musical,

00:56:27.129 --> 00:56:29.958
Love of Siam The Musical, on the 11th. It's showing

00:56:29.959 --> 00:56:30.698
until

00:56:30.699 --> 00:56:34.999
December 25th at the Thailand Ratchadalai Theatre. Please

00:56:35.760 --> 00:56:37.398
come and see it!

00:56:37.399 --> 00:56:38.799
There are so many actors,

00:56:38.800 --> 00:56:41.398
including many veteran actors.

00:56:41.399 --> 00:56:43.398
Please come and watch "Love, Competition, and Risky."

00:56:44.189 --> 00:56:46.319
Also, please support Bonnie and Five Me.

00:56:46.320 --> 00:56:49.029
As for who will be in the tape, please follow along!

00:56:49.030 --> 00:56:49.839
That's all for today.

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:52.218
Bye, Bonnie!

